.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:23255084:1","pmid":"23255084","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Akawi et al. (2013) identified 3 novel homozygous mutations in JAM3 (c.2T>G, p.M1R; c.346G>A, p.E116K; c.656G>A, p.C219Y) in 3 families of Turkish, Afghani, and Moroccan origin with HDBSCC; the mutations segregated with disease and were absent from dbSNP and 1000 Genomes.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Sequencing the coding exons of JAM3 revealed three novel homozygous mutations: c.2T>G (p.M1R), c.346G>A (p.E116K), and c.656G>A (p.C219Y).","The three mutations were not reported in dbSNP or the 1000 genome project."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23255084:2","pmid":"23255084","section":"gene_function","statement":"Akawi et al. (2013) found in transfected HeLa cells that the p.C219Y mutant, which disrupts a disulfide bond in the Ig-like C2-type domain, was retained in the endoplasmic reticulum, whereas the p.E116K mutant trafficked normally to the plasma membrane like wildtype and may have lost function through impaired interaction with a partner.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Cellular studies showed that the p.C219Y mutation resulted in a significant retention of the mutated protein in the endoplasmic reticulum, suggesting a trafficking defect.","The p.E116K mutant traffics normally to the plasma membrane as the wild-type and may have lost its function due to the lack of interaction with an interacting partner."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23255084:3","pmid":"23255084","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Akawi et al. (2013) noted that all affected children had bilateral cataracts and progressive neurologic impairment, with seizures in some, and neuroimaging showed intraparenchymal hemorrhage with brain destruction and subependymal calcification; renal anomalies and liver enlargement were more variable, and the disorder was lethal in 7 of 10 cases in the first weeks of life, with survivors severely retarded with spasticity and seizures.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["All the affected children in this report had bilateral cataracts and develop progressive neurological impairment, associated with seizures in some individuals.","The disorder was lethal in 7 out of the 10 cases. Death occurred in the first few weeks of life. Those who survived (3 in total) are severely retarded with severe spasticity and seizures."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:23255084:4","pmid":"23255084","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Akawi et al. (2013) observed that missense mutations affecting different JAM3 domains produced a similar phenotype, suggesting clinical and molecular homogeneity likely due to total loss of protein function.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Our study revealed several missense mutations scattered throughout the coding sequence of JAM3 affecting different domains yet the affected children shared similar phenotype indicating homogeneity of this disorder at the clinical and molecular level."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:21109224:1","pmid":"21109224","section":"mapping","statement":"By homozygosity mapping in a large consanguineous family from the United Arab Emirates, Mochida et al. (2010) mapped the disease locus to chromosome 11q25 with a maximum multipoint LOD score of 6.15.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Homozygosity mapping identified the disease locus in this family on chromosome 11q25 with a maximum multipoint LOD score of 6.15."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:21109224:2","pmid":"21109224","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Mochida et al. (2010) found that the JAM3 intron 5 splice-donor mutation caused abnormal splicing in a patient lymphoblast cell line by RT-PCR, leading to a frameshift with early termination.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["RT-PCR analysis of a patient lymphoblast cell line confirmed abnormal splicing, leading to a frameshift mutation with early termination."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:21109224:3","pmid":"21109224","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Mochida et al. (2010) noted that the clinical presentation overlapped with some reported cases of pseudo-TORCH syndrome and with occludin mutation cases, but massive intracranial hemorrhage distinguished these patients.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Their clinical presentation overlaps with some reported cases of pseudo-TORCH syndrome as well as with cases involving mutations in occludin, another component of the tight-junction complex. However, massive intracranial hemorrhage distinguishes these patients from others."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:21109224:4","pmid":"21109224","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Mochida et al. (2010) suggested that JAM3 is essential for maintaining the integrity of the cerebrovascular endothelium as well as for normal lens development in humans.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Our results suggest that JAM3 is essential for maintaining the integrity of the cerebrovascular endothelium as well as for normal lens development in humans."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["JAM3"],"confirmed":["JAM3"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AR"]}